# Introduction

This report analyzes survey data for four different cohorts of Unis-Cité volunteers who did their service civique in France between 2020 and 2024.

@sec-who provides information on who the volunteers are, in terms of age, sex, level of education and geographical location. @sec-attrition provides an overview on how many volunteers answered to questionnaires at different stages of their service civique (every volunteer is asked to fill out three questionnaires in total).

In @sec-within-change, we look at how volunteers have changed certain attitudes and views during their service civique, and which demographic and other factors are associated with changes.

We then look at several outcomes of interest in detail, namely whether volunteers end their contract early (@sec-rupture), how satisfied they are (@sec-satisfaction), and how confident they are about their future (@sec-perception-avenir). For these outcomes, we analyze whether there are trends across the different cohorts, and whether the outcomes are associated with demographic and other factors. 

In @sec-programs we investigate demographic differences between different key programs offered by Unis-Cité for their volunteers. 

The report ends with a brief conclusion on methodological notes for future questionnaires. 

For these analyses, we rely on questionnaires collected by Unis-Cité for four different cohorts of volunteers who did their service civique for a year (2020-2021; 2021-2022; 2022-2023; 2023-2024). These questionnaires are very extensive. For the present analyses, we selected a subset of key questions (a full list can be found in the [codebook](codebook.qmd))^[Note that the questions have been selected based on the questionnaire of the first cohort (2020/21). As a result, potentially interesting variables that only appear in later questionnaires will not appear here. In addition, a few questions whose wording changed in later cohorts could not be matched across all cohorts; the corresponding variables are missing for those cohorts.]. 

Before diving into the results, a note on caution in interpreting the results presented in this report: Whenever we speak of "predictions" that simply means statistical associations---mere observations of differences between groups. This report does **not** provide any evidence that would warrant causal conclusions---answers as to **why** we observe these differences. 

Because the data underlying this report is observational and---for most of it---does not allow to track opinion change (because most questions have only been asked once per cohort), the main objective of this report is not to provide specific and definitive answers. Instead, the aim of this report is to help Unis-Cité in identifying patterns and sparking questions that may inform new evaluation procedures. The quantitative results presented here can act as pointers, but they are not problem diagnoses. They always need to be accompanied by qualitative assessments of *why* we observe certain differences, and what could or could not be done about them. 

# Who are the volunteers ? {#sec-who}

In this section, we review some demographic variables in detail. An extensive summary table with sample demographics across the different cohorts can be found in [Tables](tables.qmd). 

## Geographic location


::: {.cell}

:::


Volunteers came from 73 different departments (see @fig-map-repartition). Note that the maps in this section only cover metropolitan France: the 541 volunteers from sites in La Réunion, as well as 19 volunteers from a site that could not be located ("Manche Nord"), are not shown. On average, across the different cohorts, most volunteers came from Bouches-du-Rhône (n = 404), followed by Nord (n = 350) and Seine-Saint-Denis (n = 316). 

Overall, there has been a steady increase in volunteers, from 6386 in 20-21 to 7848 in 23-24, and an average increase of 490.4 per year. Since the 20-21 cohort, each departement has on average increased by 19.3. There were 47 departements who saw an increase, and 15 who saw a decrease (see @fig-map-trend). For details on the trend of each departement, see [Tables](tables.qmd). 


::: {.cell}
