Number of volunteers per survey time point,  **excluding** ruptures. Volunteers who appear under 'q0' have participated in the program but have not even filled out the first questionnaire (q1). Note that in the percentage plot, the percentages are relative to all volunteers from the respective promo.
:::


## Which volunteers answer the questionnaires? A word of caution {#sec-attrition-bias}

Attrition is not only unfortunate because it means less data---it can also distort results. The volunteers who keep answering the questionnaires are not necessarily the same kind of people as those who stop answering. Imagine, as an extreme example, that only the happiest volunteers fill out the last questionnaire: results based on that questionnaire would paint a much rosier picture than reality, simply because the unhappy volunteers are missing from the data.

We can check whether something like this is going on, because we know how volunteers answered *earlier* questionnaires. The check works like this: take all volunteers who reported their satisfaction in the second questionnaire (q2), and split them into two groups---those who later answered the third questionnaire (q3), and those who did not. If the two groups gave very different answers back at q2, then the volunteers who answer q3 are not representative of everyone.


::: {.cell}

:::


@fig-attrition-bias shows the result of this check: volunteers who later answered q3 were indeed somewhat more satisfied at q2 (55% answered "Très satisfaisante") than volunteers who did not answer q3 (48%). The difference is real, but moderate. In practical terms: results based on the third questionnaire---for example, the changes in perceptions in @sec-individual-action---probably paint a slightly too positive picture, but they are unlikely to be fundamentally misleading. We ran the same check one questionnaire earlier (comparing the q1 answers of volunteers who did and did not answer q2) and also found only small differences there.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Share of volunteers who reported being very satisfied ('Très satisfaisante') in the second questionnaire (q2), split by whether they later answered the third questionnaire (q3) or not. If the two bars were equal, the volunteers who answer q3 would be perfectly representative---at least in terms of satisfaction---of all volunteers who answered q2.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-attrition-bias-1.png){#fig-attrition-bias}
:::
:::


# How have volunteers changed their attitudes? {#sec-within-change}

In the selection of variables made for this report, there are only two questions that volunteers have been asked at several time points during their service civique: 

1. "Avez-vous voté lors des dernières élections (locales ou nationales) ?" (@sec-vote)

2. "En général, pensez-vous que votre action individuelle peut contribuer à changer la société ?" (@sec-individual-action). 

For all analyses in this section, keep in mind that they rely only on the minority of volunteers who answered at *both* relevant time points (see @sec-attrition-bias for what this implies).

## Voting {#sec-vote}

In this section, we look at how volunteers have changed regarding their voting behavior. Voting behavior was measured with the question: "Avez-vous voté lors des dernières élections (locales ou nationales) ?", which has been asked at time points q1 (at the beginning of the service civique) and q2 (at its end). @fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview shows that, averaged across all cohorts and time points, the majority of volunteers reports having voted.


::: {#fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview .cell layout-ncol="2"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![(absolute numbers)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview-1.png){#fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview-1}
:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![(percentages)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview-2.png){#fig-vote_derniers_elections-overview-2}
:::

Distribution of whether volunteers reported having voted or not during the last elections, averaged across time points and cohorts.

:::



In the following sections, we investigate how volunteers have changed in answering this question, throughout their service civique. For these analyses, we only consider those volunteers who have answered the question at both time points, q1 and q2. Changes towards "Yes" or "No" can have happened either from the respective other option, or from the third answer option: "Vous n'aviez pas l'âge de voter ou vous n'étiez pas inscrit.e sur les listes électorales", which is abbreviated in the plots below as "Pas sur les listes". We look at the different cohorts separately (promo 2020-21, @fig-vote_derniers_elections-2020; promo 2021-22, @fig-vote_derniers_elections-2021; 2022-23, @fig-vote_derniers_elections-2022; 2023-24, @fig-vote_derniers_elections-2023). 

Before turning to the differences between cohorts, note that in every cohort, most volunteers reported the same voting behavior at both time points. How many varies considerably between cohorts, however: from 57% in 2021-22 to 88% in 2022-23. The changes discussed in the following concern the remaining minority.

That said, the following sections reveal considerable differences between the cohorts. For example, in 2020-21 and 2021-22, many volunteers reported changes in voting behavior, but in opposite directions: In 2020-21, 24% of volunteers changed towards "No", outweighing those who changed to "Yes" (6%) by far. Inversely, in 2021-22, 35% of volunteers changed towards "Yes", while only 8% changed towards "No". A plausible explanation for the shift towards "Yes" in 2021-22 is that 2022---the year in which this cohort filled out their second questionnaire---was the year of a presidential election.

More generally, one explanation for the differences between cohorts might be that volunteers care more about national elections than local ones (or vice versa). If that is the case, we would expect changes in voting behavior to depend on whether there were national or local elections happening during the year of a cohort's service civique. For reference, France's election calendar during the period covered by this report was as follows: municipal elections in spring 2020, regional elections in June 2021, presidential elections in April 2022 followed by legislative elections in June 2022, no nationwide elections in 2023, and European elections followed by snap legislative elections in June--July 2024. In the sections below, we indicate for each cohort which elections had taken place before each questionnaire. The 2022-23 cohort illustrates the point well: no nationwide elections took place during their service year, and this cohort was by far the most stable in its answers (88% reported the same voting behavior at both time points).

### Promo 2020-21

@fig-vote_derniers_elections-2020 shows change in volunteers' reported voting behavior for the promo "2020-21". For this cohort, the last elections before Q1 were the municipal elections of spring 2020; the regional elections of June 2021 took place around the time volunteers filled out Q2.


