## Individual action for society {#sec-individual-action}

In this section, we look at how volunteers have changed regarding their perception on whether their individual action can contribute to changing society. This was measured with the question: "En général, pensez-vous que votre action individuelle peut contribuer à changer la société ?", which has been asked at time points q1 (at the beginning of the service civique) and q3 (six months after its end). We look at the different cohorts separately (promo 2020-21, @fig-action_individuelle_societe-2020; promo 2021-22, @fig-action_individuelle_societe-2021; 2022-23, @fig-action_individuelle_societe-2022; 2023-24, @fig-action_individuelle_societe-2023).

Descriptively, there is no clear positive or negative trend. The main takeaway is rather one of stability: a majority of volunteers (65% of those who answered at both time points) already think at the beginning of their service civique that their individual action can contribute to changing society, and most of them (77%) still think so six months after its end. This is perhaps not surprising: young people who choose to do a service civique probably tend to believe from the outset that their action can be useful. Among the minority who do change their answer, on average, across all cohorts, slightly more people change towards "No" or "Not really", compared to "Yes".

