# Rupture {#sec-rupture}


::: {.cell}

:::


Not all volunteers work until the end of their contract. In fact, 22.9% of volunteers have a "rupture", i.e. terminate the contract early (@fig-rupture-overview). There are various motives for ending one's contract early (@fig-rupture-motive). Not all of them are necessarily bad, e.g. "Embauche en CDD d'au moins 6 mois ou CDI", and some are outside of the influence of the volunteers, e.g. "Fin de validité du Titre de Séjour". 


::: {#fig-rupture-overview .cell layout-ncol="2"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![(absolute numbers)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-overview-1.png){#fig-rupture-overview-1}
:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![(percentages)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-overview-2.png){#fig-rupture-overview-2}
:::

Number of volunteers with a rupture (for various possible reasons, including positive ones, such as obtaining a work contract), pooled across all cohorts.
:::




::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Prevalence of different rupture motives, pooled across all cohorts.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-motive-1.png){#fig-rupture-motive}
:::
:::



## When do ruptures happen?

Ruptures do not all happen at the same moment. @fig-rupture-timing shows, for all volunteers who ended their contract early, how many months of service they had completed at that point. Ruptures happen throughout the whole year, but disproportionately early on: about a third of the volunteers who have a rupture leave within their first three months, and half leave within their first five months (while most volunteers sign up for eight months). For Unis-Cité, this suggests that support aimed at preventing (negative) ruptures matters most in the first months of the service civique.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Number of months of service civique completed before the rupture, among all volunteers with a rupture (pooled across all cohorts, all rupture motives combined).](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-timing-1.png){#fig-rupture-timing}
:::
:::


## Change in rupture

@fig-rupture illustrates change regarding how many volunteers have ended their contract early (rupture) by comparing the different promos. There are no clear trends with slightly more than 20% of volunteers having a rupture.


::: {#fig-rupture .cell layout-ncol="2"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![(absolute numbers)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-1.png){#fig-rupture-1}
:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![(percentages)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-2.png){#fig-rupture-2}
:::

Number of volunteers with a rupture (for various possible reasons, including positive ones, such as obtaining a work contract).
:::


## What predicts whether volunteers end their contract early (rupture)?

Statistical models on rupture can be found in the Appendix (@sec-appendix-rupture). Here, we only report the variables that were statistically significant in these models. Not all ruptures happen for negative reasons (@fig-rupture-motive). For analyses in this section, we focus on negative reasons: We distinguish between volunteers who ended their contract early for apparently *negative* reasons, and all other volunteers. 


::: {.cell}

:::


### Demographic factors

As shown in @fig-rupture-descriptive, older volunteers tend to have less ruptures than younger ones; less educated volunteers (in particular "infra bac") tend to have more ruptures than those with higher education; refugees tend to have less ruptures than non-refugees; male volunteers tend to have slightly more ruptures than female ones; "RELAIS"-type volunteers tend to have slightly fewer ruptures than "CŒUR"-type volunteers (these are the two forms of volunteer status at Unis-Cité, coded in the variable `type_volontaire`); volunteers from "QPV" residential areas tend to have considerably higher rupture rates than volunteers from other residential areas. 

Note, however, that these groups overlap: for example, volunteers from "QPV" areas are also less educated on average. When all demographic factors are taken into account at the same time (@sec-appendix-multivariable), the higher rupture rate of volunteers from "QPV" areas disappears almost entirely---it is largely explained by differences in education. Education itself remains the strongest predictor of ruptures.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Percentages of rupture for (allegedly) negative reasons for different groups, for different variables.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-descriptive-1.png){#fig-rupture-descriptive}
:::
:::


### Other factors

For non-demographic variables, investigating their relationship with rupture is not possible--simply because, by definition, for questions that have been only asked at "q2" and "q3", volunteers who had ended their contract early were not available anymore (see @tbl-rupture-candidates). Only for the two variables that have been asked at "q1": 

1. `perception_avenir` (Concernant votre avenir, êtes-vous...? 	Très confiant(e); Assez confiant(e); Peu confiant(e); Pas du tout confiant(e))
2. `action_individuelle_societe` (En général, pensez-vous que votre action individuelle peut contribuer à changer la société ? 	Oui; Pas vraiment; Non)

As shown in @fig-rupture-other, perhaps unsurprisingly, those volunteers who tend to think that their individual action can contribute to changing society tend to end their contract early less often. Interestingly, volunteers who are very confident regarding their future tend to end their contract early more often. Note that this analysis only concerns ruptures for apparently *negative* reasons---so this pattern is not simply explained by confident volunteers leaving for positive reasons, such as finding a job.



::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Percentages of rupture for (allegedly) negative reasons, as a function of (i) the volunteers' confidence in their future and (ii) their perception that their individual action can contribute to changing society.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-rupture-other-1.png){#fig-rupture-other}
:::
:::


# Satisfaction {#sec-satisfaction}

In this section, we look at satisfaction ("D'une manière générale, diriez-vous que votre Service Civique s'est déroulé de façon..." with levels 1, "pas du tout satisfaisante", to 4, "très satisfaisante")^[In all analyses we treat this as a continuous variable]. As shown in @fig-satisfaction-overview, taking all cohorts together, the majority of volunteers thinks their experience is "très satisfaisant". 


::: {#fig-satisfaction-overview .cell layout-ncol="2"}
