![(percentages)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-overview-2.png){#fig-satisfaction-overview-2}
:::

Distribution of satisfaction levels, pooled across all cohorts.
:::


## Change in Satisfaction

@fig-satisfaction shows how satisfaction with the service civique has changed over time, between the different cohorts. The share of volunteers who are "very satisfied" has decreased over time, but the shares of those who are "not" or "little" satisfied have remained broadly stable. 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Satisfaction between cohorts.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-1.png){#fig-satisfaction}
:::
:::


## Is the decline in satisfaction driven by changing demographics? {#sec-satisfaction-composition}


::: {.cell}

:::


The composition of volunteers has changed considerably across cohorts: among volunteers who answered the satisfaction question, the share of minors (16-17) has almost doubled (from roughly 12% in 2020-21 to roughly 22% in 2023-24), and the share of volunteers without a bac has increased (from roughly 27% to 38%). Since younger and less educated volunteers tend to report lower satisfaction, one might suspect that the declining share of "very satisfied" volunteers simply reflects this changing composition, rather than a change in the experience of the service civique itself.

The data suggest that this is not the case: as shown in @fig-satisfaction-composition, the share of very satisfied volunteers has declined *within* practically all age groups and all education levels. In line with this, in a linear regression of satisfaction on cohort (coded 1 to 4), the estimated trend is essentially unchanged when adjusting for age and education (-0.033 points per cohort unadjusted, vs. -0.03 adjusted). The decline in satisfaction therefore does not appear to be explained by the changing demographic composition of volunteers.


::: {#fig-satisfaction-composition .cell layout-ncol="2"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![(age groups)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-composition-1.png){#fig-satisfaction-composition-1}
:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![(education levels)](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-composition-2.png){#fig-satisfaction-composition-2}
:::

Share of volunteers who report being 'very satisfied' ('Très satisfaisante'), by cohort, separately for age groups and education levels. If the overall decline were only driven by the changing composition of volunteers, the trends within groups would be flat.
:::


## What predicts whether volunteers are more satisfied ?

Statistical models on satisfaction can be found in the Appendix (@sec-appendix-satisfaction). Here, we only report the variables that were statistically significant in these models. 


::: {.cell}

:::


### Demographic factors

As shown in @fig-satisfaction-descriptive, older volunteers tend to be more satisfied than younger ones; more educated volunteers tend to be more satisfied than less educated; "RELAIS"-type volunteers tend to be more satisfied than "CŒUR"-type volunteers; probably unsurprisingly, volunteers who have a rupture tend to be less satisfied than volunteers who don't. When all demographic factors are considered at once (@sec-appendix-multivariable), the differences by education disappear (they mostly reflect age differences), while the differences by age and by volunteer type remain.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Share of volunteers responding to the different satisfaction levels, grouped by different variables.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-descriptive-1.png){#fig-satisfaction-descriptive}
:::
:::


### Other factors

The following variables are statistically associated with satisfaction (@fig-satisfaction-other): 

- `comparaison_utilite_autres` ["Diriez-vous que votre Service Civique a été...? 	Plus utile à vous qu'aux autres; Autant utile à vous qu'aux autres; Moins utile à vous qu'aux autres"]. Probably unsurprisingly, volunteers who believe the service civique was less useful for them than for others tend to be less satisfied than those who think it was more useful to them 
- `fierte` ["Vous sentez-vous fier de ce que vous avez accompli pendant votre Service Civique ? 	Oui, tout à fait; Oui, un peu; Non, pas vraiment; Non, pas du tout"]. Volunteers who are more proud with what they have achieved during their service civique also tend to be more satisfied. 
- `confiance_en_soi` ["Pensez-vous avoir gagné en confiance en vous durant votre Service Civique ? 	Oui, beaucoup; Oui, assez; Non, pas vraiment; Non, pas du tout; Je ne sais pas"] Volunteers who have gained confidence also tend to be more satisfied.
- `confiance_avenir_personnel` ["Concernant votre avenir personnel, êtes-vous...? 	Très confiant.e; Assez confiant.e; Peu confiant.e; Pas du tout confiant.e"] Volunteers who are more confident about their future also tend to be more satisfied. 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Share of volunteers responding to the different satisfaction levels, grouped by different variables.](report.en_files/figure-docx/fig-satisfaction-other-1.png){#fig-satisfaction-other}
:::
:::


