## Looking at all factors at once {#sec-appendix-multivariable}

All models so far look at one factor at a time. This is easy to interpret, but it has a limit: the groups overlap. For example, volunteers from "QPV" areas are also less educated on average, and older volunteers tend to be more educated. So when we observe that volunteers from "QPV" areas have more ruptures, we cannot tell whether this is about where they live or about their level of education.

To help disentangle this, we can put all demographic factors into a *single* model (a "multivariable" model). The estimates then answer a slightly different question: "Comparing volunteers who are similar on all the *other* factors, does this factor still make a difference?" We ran such a model for three outcomes: negative ruptures (@fig-multivariable-rupture), satisfaction (@fig-multivariable-satisfaction) and confidence in one's future (@fig-multivariable-perception-avenir). The plots read exactly like the corresponding one-factor-at-a-time plots above.


::: {.cell}

:::


Three results stand out:

- For ruptures (@fig-multivariable-rupture), the "QPV" difference disappears: comparing volunteers with the same level of education, age, sex, and so on, living in a "QPV" area makes almost no difference anymore (the odds ratio is very close to 1). The higher rupture rate of "QPV" volunteers seen in @fig-rupture-descriptive is thus largely explained by differences in education. Education itself remains the strongest predictor: all else equal, volunteers with a bac or more have considerably fewer negative ruptures. The age differences also shrink considerably---older volunteers have fewer ruptures mostly *because* they are more educated.
- For satisfaction (@fig-multivariable-satisfaction), the opposite happens: the differences by education disappear, while the differences by age remain. Older volunteers are more satisfied, at every level of education.
- For confidence in one's future (@fig-multivariable-perception-avenir), the differences by sex, by refugee status and by volunteer type remain clearly visible, while education and age make little difference once the other factors are taken into account.

Two notes of caution. First, these models can only include volunteers for whom *all* factors are known (for example, N = 14,927 volunteers for the rupture model)---volunteers with missing information on any factor are left out. Second, as everywhere in this report, these are associations: "all else equal" refers only to the factors in the model, not to everything that could possibly matter.


::: {.cell}
